<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#measles">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Measles</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Measles is a highly contagious viral disease. Introduced to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> by Europeans, it caused devastating epidemics among indigenous populations who had no immunity. Along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> and other diseases, it contributed to the catastrophic population decline that accompanied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>'s conquests.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/measles.jpg" alt="Measles" width="320" height="491" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CDC/NIP/Barbara Rice · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RougeoleDP.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Epidemics of smallpox, <b>measles</b> and the diseases known as cocoliztli, especially in 1545–1548 and 1576–1580, together with war and forced labour, reduced the indigenous population of central Mexico by perhaps 90 per cent by the early seventeenth century, although the size of the original population is disputed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Eurasian diseases including smallpox, <b>measles</b> and typhus killed an estimated 50 to 90 per cent of the pre-contact indigenous population within a century of contact, one of the most catastrophic demographic events in human history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Diseases brought by whalers and explorers caused mass death: in the 19th century the Western Arctic lost close to 90 per cent of its people to tuberculosis, <b>measles</b>, influenza and smallpox.</blockquote><a href="/culture/inuit">Inuit · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Epidemics of influenza, <b>measles</b> and smallpox killed between 10 and 50 per cent of Māori, and their numbers may have fallen from about 100,000 in 1800 to 50,000–80,000 by 1843.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maori">Māori · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Many historians blame epidemics of smallpox, influenza, <b>measles</b> and typhus above all; others, such as Andrés Reséndez, argue that enslavement and forced labour in the mines were a major killer, and David Stannard calls the encomienda a genocidal system.</blockquote><a href="/culture/taino">Taíno · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Bertrand Russell argued that it weakens the individual’s capacity to judge his country’s foreign policy; Albert Einstein called it “an infantile disease … the <b>measles</b> of mankind”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
